      "sequence_position": 3,
      "problem": "Use mathematical induction to prove that ${n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n$ for $n\\in N.$",
      "worked_solution": "The principle of induction states the following: Suppose we have a sequence of mathematical statements $P(1),P(2),P(3),…,$ where\n\n- $P(1)$ is true, and\n\n- $P(k)⇒P(k+1)$ for every $k\\in N.$\n\nThen, $P(n)$ is true for every $n\\in N.$\n\nIn this case, the general statement $P(n)$ we're required to prove is the following:\n\n$$\n{n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n\\,\\text{for}\\,n\\ge 1.\n$$\n\nWe start by proving the so-called base case, $P(1).$ To do this, we substitute $n=1$ into both the left-hand side and right-hand side and show that the left-hand side is greater than or equal to the right-hand side:\n\nWe proceed using induction on $n.$\n\n**Base Case**: We first prove our statement is true for the base case $n=1.$\nFor the left-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{1}^{3}+{1}^{2}=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nand for the right-hand side, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(1)=2,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, our statement is true for $n=1.$\n\nNext, we state our inductive hypothesis. Here, we assume that the given statement is true for some $n=k\\ge 1.$ \n\n**Inductive Hypothesis**: We assume that for some $n=k\\ge 1,$ we have\n\n$$\n{k}^{3}+{k}^{2}\\ge 2k.\n$$\n\nWe must show that $P(k)⇒P(k+1).$ Let's start by writing down $P(k+1).$ This is the first part of the so-called **inductive step**.\n\n**Inductive Step**: We're required to prove that\n\n$$\n(k+1)^{3}+(k+1)^{2}\\ge 2(k+1).\\,\\,(∗)\n$$\n\nThe idea is to rewrite the left-hand side of $(∗)$ *using the inductive hypothesis* and apply algebraic techniques to show that this is greater than or equal to the right-hand side. We do this as follows:\n\nWe write\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}(k+1)^{3}+(k+1)^{2} & =({k}^{3}+3{k}^{2}+3k+1)+({k}^{2}+2k+1) \\\\  & ={k}^{3}+{k}^{2}+3{k}^{2}+5k+2.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nBy the inductive hypothesis, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{{{k}^{3}+{k}^{2}}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 2k}+{{3{k}^{2}+5k}_{⏟}}_{\\ge 0}+2 & \\ge 2k+2=2(k+1),\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nas required.\n\nSince we've shown that the left-hand side of $(∗)$ is greater than or equal to the right-hand side, our proof is complete. So, let's write down our conclusion. \n\nTherefore, by the principle of induction, ${n}^{3}+{n}^{2}\\ge 2n$ for all $n\\ge 1.$",
